---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';

const description =
  'Dastan Aitzhanov on a path from cloud data and AI platforms to independent systems work, ownership, and governance.';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description={description}
>
  <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
  <div class="prose about-prose">
    <p>
      I build AI systems and write about what happens when they are allowed to act. The question that
      connects the work is simple: who is allowed to decide, and who answers for the result?
    </p>

    <h2>Trajectory</h2>
    <p>
      At AWS and Databricks, I worked on data and AI platforms: getting data somewhere a system could
      use it, then making that system dependable enough for an ordinary day. The public work I keep
      here includes industrial machine-learning architectures, human-review loops, and the operating
      paths around models.
    </p>
    <p>
      I now work independently on systems that place capable models inside explicit boundaries. The
      experiments range from local AI and structured retrieval to personal systems built around
      durable private context, observable actions, and authority that can be withdrawn. I publish the
      methods and results that hold up.
    </p>
    <p>
      That technical direction is shaped by ownership. Before technology, I ran my family's
      commercial real estate business in Kazakhstan; I still serve on its board and have since
      invested in real estate projects in Texas. I also ran a quantitative fund that now manages only
      family capital. Those roles made the cost of a decision concrete: analysis can be delegated,
      but responsibility for the outcome cannot.
    </p>

    <h2>Working principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/ideas/policy-should-not-be-probabilistic/">Policy should not be probabilistic.</a>{' '}
        A model can recommend an action. The rule that permits it should be code someone can read,
        version, and test.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ideas/autonomy-should-scale-with-reversibility/">Autonomy should scale with reversibility.</a>{' '}
        The useful question is not whether an agent is autonomous, but how much autonomy a particular
        action can bear.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ideas/an-audit-log-is-not-a-control/">An audit log is not a control.</a>{' '}
        A record written after an action is evidence. It cannot stop the action.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/ideas/novelty-belongs-to-models/">Novelty belongs to models. Repetition belongs to software.</a>{' '}
        Use a model to find a path the first time. Once the path repeats, call it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/writing/the-computer-should-be-personal/">A person's thinking should live near the person.</a>{' '}
        Private context is the asset. Where it is allowed to travel is the design.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/writing/what-owners-notice/">Analysis is becoming cheap. Commitment is not.</a>{' '}
        The person who authorizes a decision still carries the consequence after the analysis is done.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Writing and contact</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/writing/">Writing</a> holds developed arguments. <a href="/ideas/">Ideas</a> is the
      atomic notebook: shorter claims, the reasons I hold them, and where they might be wrong. I write
      when I have something definite enough to defend. I live in Texas.
    </p>
    <p>
      Email is the way to reach me:{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      Code I have published lives on <a href={site.github} rel="me">GitHub</a>, and selected public
      work is collected on the <a href="/work/">Work page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
